1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:10,157
Welcome to ESP32 Circuit Lab. In this practical walkthrough, we
will build a blinking LED, move it to a different GPIO, use

2
00:00:10,157 --> 00:00:15,035
a pushbutton as an input, and control brightness with a
potentiometer.

3
00:00:15,035 --> 00:00:23,352
We will also inspect a waveform, experiment with a circuit that
needs no code, and back up a project.

4
00:00:23,352 --> 00:00:28,231
Everything shown is a virtual circuit. No physical board is
required.

5
00:00:28,231 --> 00:00:32,469
This recording uses the live website and separate demonstration
storage.

6
00:00:33,847 --> 00:00:39,618
The center is the circuit workspace. The right panel switches
between Parts and Properties.

7
00:00:39,618 --> 00:00:48,425
Selecting a component shows its values and electrical readouts.
Code and output live in the lower drawer, and Run is at the

8
00:00:48,425 --> 00:00:53,437
top. Fit brings the circuit back into view after zooming or
panning.

9
00:00:53,437 --> 00:01:02,320
Examples are useful starting points. An example opens read only;
making a copy, or editing it, gives you your own project

10
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:04,446
instead of changing the original.

11
00:01:05,753 --> 00:01:11,138
Run as selects the execution engine. JavaScript runs in this
browser.

12
00:01:11,138 --> 00:01:20,054
Circuit only runs no sketch. Arduino C plus plus compiles on the
server and runs on a virtual ESP32.

13
00:01:20,054 --> 00:01:26,762
The choices are not language converters. Arduino sketches use
void setup and void loop.

14
00:01:26,762 --> 00:01:33,913
Browser JavaScript uses function declarations against the circuit
solver; it is not ESP32 machine code.

15
00:01:33,913 --> 00:01:37,267
Choose the matching mode before pressing Run.

16
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:47,724
For the first scenario, open the menu, choose Getting started,
and select Start guided Blink.

17
00:01:47,724 --> 00:01:56,124
This creates a fresh project with an ESP32 and a starter sketch.
The five-step guide begins with the components, then checks

18
00:01:56,124 --> 00:02:01,345
the wiring, explains the code, and finally watches for real LED
activity.

19
00:02:01,345 --> 00:02:06,491
You can dismiss the guide at any time and reopen Getting started
later.

20
00:02:06,491 --> 00:02:10,729
For our first build, leave the GPIO selector at two.

21
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:21,317
Add an LED and a resistor from the Parts panel. The resistor must
be in series with the LED; simply placing it nearby does

22
00:02:21,317 --> 00:02:27,958
not limit current. For this demonstration we use the starter
resistor value of two hundred twenty ohms.

23
00:02:27,958 --> 00:02:34,527
The LED has an anode, marked A, and a cathode, marked C. Its
direction matters.

24
00:02:34,527 --> 00:02:40,804
Fit the view if needed, then look for D2 and a ground pin on the
ESP32.

25
00:02:42,204 --> 00:02:47,134
Create a wire by clicking its starting pin and then its
destination.

26
00:02:47,134 --> 00:02:53,473
Connect D2 to one resistor terminal. Connect the other resistor
terminal to the LED anode.

27
00:02:53,473 --> 00:03:01,846
Finally, connect the LED cathode to ground. Either resistor
orientation works, but the LED must face the right way.

28
00:03:01,846 --> 00:03:06,776
Notice that the guide advances only after finding a valid series
path.

29
00:03:06,776 --> 00:03:13,897
A wire that bypasses the resistor does not satisfy this check,
even if the drawing looks close.

30
00:03:15,262 --> 00:03:23,177
Open code to read the explanation alongside the sketch. On this
ESP32, LED BUILTIN refers to GPIO two.

31
00:03:23,177 --> 00:03:28,213
Setup runs once and configures the pin as an output using
pinMode.

32
00:03:28,213 --> 00:03:34,929
Loop then repeats: digitalWrite with HIGH drives the output high,
and LOW drives it low.

33
00:03:34,929 --> 00:03:40,205
A delay of one thousand means one thousand milliseconds, or one
second.

34
00:03:40,205 --> 00:03:46,042
One second on followed by one second off gives a complete two-
second blink cycle.

35
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:53,292
Continue to Run, confirm Arduino C plus plus is selected, and
press Run.

36
00:03:53,292 --> 00:04:01,280
Compilation time depends on the server and its cache, so wait for
the running state rather than clicking repeatedly.

37
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:06,104
This demonstration uses the real firmware backend, not a mock
response.

38
00:04:06,104 --> 00:04:14,487
Watch the external LED turn on and off. The guide marks Blink as
working only after it observes both states.

39
00:04:14,487 --> 00:04:19,074
The ESP32's blue IO2 indicator is separate from the external LED.

40
00:04:20,620 --> 00:04:28,558
Now let us use a pin other than two. Stop the sketch first,
reopen Review code, and select GPIO eighteen.

41
00:04:28,558 --> 00:04:36,425
The guide updates its instructions and completion checks, but it
does not rewrite your sketch or move a wire for you.

42
00:04:36,425 --> 00:04:41,670
This is important: the selector is a learning aid, not automatic
circuit conversion.

43
00:04:41,670 --> 00:04:50,033
GPIO eighteen is labelled D18 on this board. We must change both
the physical connection in the drawing and the pin used by

44
00:04:50,033 --> 00:04:50,955
the code.

45
00:04:52,368 --> 00:05:00,875
Keep the resistor, LED, and ground connections unchanged. Only
the ESP32 end of the resistor feed needs to change.

46
00:05:00,875 --> 00:05:09,700
In this demonstration, we remove the old D2 feed wire and draw a
replacement from D18 to the same resistor terminal.

47
00:05:09,700 --> 00:05:18,366
The selected GPIO must match this wire. Once the new path is
correct, the guide returns to the code step.

48
00:05:18,366 --> 00:05:25,761
Changing the selection also clears the previous completion
evidence, so an earlier successful blink cannot pass a different

49
00:05:25,761 --> 00:05:26,397
pin.

50
00:05:27,801 --> 00:05:32,579
Declare a constant called LED PIN with the value eighteen above
setup.

51
00:05:32,579 --> 00:05:36,978
Use that same constant in pinMode and in both digitalWrite calls.

52
00:05:36,978 --> 00:05:43,879
The complete reference example shows exactly where it belongs.
You do not need to redefine LED BUILTIN.

53
00:05:43,879 --> 00:05:53,056
A named constant keeps future changes in one place. Here we also
change both delays to five hundred, giving half a second on

54
00:05:53,056 --> 00:05:58,820
and half a second off. Keep Arduino C plus plus selected for this
code.

55
00:06:00,230 --> 00:06:08,688
Run the updated sketch and watch the external LED. It now blinks
from GPIO eighteen, even though the built-in IO2 indicator

56
00:06:08,688 --> 00:06:17,680
belongs to GPIO two. The guide verifies the chosen pin is
configured as an output and that the LED actually turns on and

57
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:23,471
off through the resistor. The GPIO selectors are disabled while a
sketch is running.

58
00:06:23,471 --> 00:06:32,234
Stop before changing them. For another output pin later, update
the constant, move the feed wire, and repeat this check.

59
00:06:33,611 --> 00:06:43,203
If an LED does not blink, check three things together: the
guide's selected GPIO, the pin number in the sketch, and the

60
00:06:43,203 --> 00:06:49,886
actual wire endpoint. Then check OUTPUT initialization, LED
direction, ground, and the resistor path.

61
00:06:49,886 --> 00:06:58,221
Pins thirty-four through thirty-nine are input only on this ESP32
and cannot drive an LED, so the guide excludes them.

62
00:06:58,221 --> 00:07:06,555
Pins one and three share USB serial. Start with eighteen when you
want a straightforward alternate output for this exercise.

63
00:07:07,938 --> 00:07:14,544
For a digital input scenario, stop the current run and open
Examples, then Button plus LED.

64
00:07:14,544 --> 00:07:20,706
This circuit uses GPIO two for the button input and GPIO thirteen
for the LED output.

65
00:07:20,706 --> 00:07:25,753
A ten-kilohm pull-down resistor keeps the input low when the
switch is released.

66
00:07:25,753 --> 00:07:30,059
Pressing the button connects the input to three point three
volts.

67
00:07:30,059 --> 00:07:37,927
This example is already wired, which lets us focus on the
difference between reading an input and driving an output.

68
00:07:40,996 --> 00:07:48,387
Read the sketch before running it. Setup configures the LED pin
as OUTPUT and the button pin as INPUT.

69
00:07:48,387 --> 00:07:57,315
Loop reads the button with digitalRead. If the result is HIGH,
the code turns the LED on; otherwise it turns it off.

70
00:07:57,315 --> 00:08:01,779
This is level-sensitive behavior: the LED follows the current
button state.

71
00:08:01,779 --> 00:08:10,414
It is not a toggle that remembers each press. The example also
assigns the letter B as a convenient keyboard control for the

72
00:08:10,414 --> 00:08:11,365
virtual button.

73
00:08:12,942 --> 00:08:19,824
Run this example in Arduino C plus plus mode. Press and hold the
virtual button, then release it.

74
00:08:19,824 --> 00:08:25,942
You can also hold B when focus is on the circuit rather than
inside the code editor.

75
00:08:25,942 --> 00:08:34,354
Observe the LED and its current changing with the input. If
typing in code appears to do nothing to the circuit, that is

76
00:08:34,354 --> 00:08:37,552
intentional: editing controls keep their normal keyboard
behavior.

77
00:08:37,552 --> 00:08:40,541
Stop the run when you finish the experiment.

78
00:08:42,062 --> 00:08:51,859
Next, open Pot to PWM, the JavaScript sketch example. Notice that
Run as now says JavaScript, and the code uses function

79
00:08:51,859 --> 00:08:58,893
setup and function loop. The potentiometer's wiper feeds GPIO
thirty-four, which is suitable for input.

80
00:08:58,893 --> 00:09:06,261
The code reads an analog value and uses it to set the PWM output
on GPIO twenty-five.

81
00:09:06,261 --> 00:09:14,886
This demonstration runs locally in the browser. Do not copy this
JavaScript syntax unchanged into the Arduino C plus plus

82
00:09:14,886 --> 00:09:15,556
mode.

83
00:09:16,916 --> 00:09:24,065
Run the sketch, hover over the potentiometer knob, and use the
mouse wheel to change its position.

84
00:09:24,065 --> 00:09:28,477
Open Serial to watch the reported reading and duty percentage
change.

85
00:09:28,477 --> 00:09:36,767
Compare those numbers with the LED's brightness. In browser mode,
PWM is modelled as an average drive, so the visible

86
00:09:36,767 --> 00:09:44,829
response also depends on the LED's voltage threshold. Use both
the numeric output and the circuit, rather than expecting the

87
00:09:44,829 --> 00:09:48,479
knob position alone to be a direct brightness percentage.

88
00:09:52,386 --> 00:10:00,595
A waveform makes changing signals easier to understand. For this
scope exercise, reopen Blink and use a small JavaScript

89
00:10:00,595 --> 00:10:07,748
sketch that toggles GPIO two every half second. We explicitly
select JavaScript because this version uses function

90
00:10:07,748 --> 00:10:13,925
declarations and runs in the browser. The circuit still uses the
real electronics solver.

91
00:10:13,925 --> 00:10:21,891
The purpose here is to inspect voltage over time, not to
demonstrate a second firmware compilation or claim that

92
00:10:21,891 --> 00:10:24,979
JavaScript is running on an ESP32 processor.

93
00:10:26,887 --> 00:10:34,715
Open Scope, choose Arm probe, and click D2 on the circuit. The
selected channel now follows that pin.

94
00:10:34,715 --> 00:10:39,780
Autoset helps fit the display, and Timebase controls the
horizontal time scale.

95
00:10:39,780 --> 00:10:47,147
With a half-second HIGH and a half-second LOW, expect a repeating
square wave with a one-second full cycle.

96
00:10:47,147 --> 00:10:55,281
The scope's own Run and Stop control freezes the display; the
main Run control starts or stops the simulation itself.

97
00:10:55,281 --> 00:10:57,046
These are different operations.

98
00:10:59,203 --> 00:11:06,391
The system also handles circuits without a microcontroller. Here
we import a prepared voltage-divider project: a five-volt

99
00:11:06,391 --> 00:11:15,633
source, two equal resistors, and ground. Run as is Circuit only,
so no sketch is executed and no firmware compilation is

100
00:11:15,633 --> 00:11:20,847
needed. The resistor in the lower half connects the midpoint to
ground.

101
00:11:20,847 --> 00:11:29,220
Select it to inspect the pin voltages and current. With equal
resistor values, the midpoint should be approximately half of

102
00:11:29,220 --> 00:11:30,642
the source voltage.

103
00:11:32,175 --> 00:11:38,806
Change the lower resistor from one thousand to two thousand ohms
and commit the field by leaving it.

104
00:11:38,806 --> 00:11:44,725
The midpoint rises from about two point five volts to about three
point three three volts.

105
00:11:44,725 --> 00:11:49,217
A larger lower resistor makes the midpoint closer to the supply
voltage.

106
00:11:49,217 --> 00:11:56,989
This is a useful way to build intuition: change one parameter,
inspect the solved result, and compare it with your

107
00:11:56,989 --> 00:12:02,979
prediction. These are idealized virtual measurements, not a
replacement for checking a real hardware design.

108
00:12:04,766 --> 00:12:13,151
Before leaving, open Projects and check your work. Saved locally
means this browser has a stored copy; it does not

109
00:12:13,151 --> 00:12:21,535
automatically mean a server or account backup exists. A private
window, a different device, or clearing site data can remove

110
00:12:21,535 --> 00:12:27,381
access to that local copy. Explicit Save is available in the menu
as well.

111
00:12:27,381 --> 00:12:32,843
In this recording, server copy stays unchecked and account sign-
in is not demonstrated.

112
00:12:32,843 --> 00:12:36,920
The demo projects are separate from any existing user projects.

113
00:12:38,585 --> 00:12:46,433
Close the project list and choose Download from the menu. The
application exports the current project as a JSON file

114
00:12:46,433 --> 00:12:53,748
containing the circuit and sketch data. Keep this file somewhere
you control, especially before clearing browser storage or

115
00:12:53,748 --> 00:13:00,911
making a major change. It is a project backup, not a firmware
binary or a video recording.

116
00:13:00,911 --> 00:13:05,331
A clear project name makes the downloaded filename easier to
recognize.

117
00:13:05,331 --> 00:13:10,893
Exporting is also a simple way to share an editable circuit with
someone else.

118
00:13:12,301 --> 00:13:19,656
To restore a backup, choose Import and select the JSON file. The
circuit reopens in My Projects.

119
00:13:19,656 --> 00:13:25,290
Check the component values, wires, code, and execution mode
before running it.

120
00:13:25,290 --> 00:13:29,437
Here the two-thousand-ohm resistor and Circuit only mode are
preserved.

121
00:13:29,437 --> 00:13:36,323
Importing into this demonstration browser does not change
somebody else's browser storage or update the public example.

122
00:13:36,323 --> 00:13:44,773
For important work, verify a backup by reopening it instead of
assuming that a downloaded file must contain the version you

123
00:13:44,773 --> 00:13:45,399
intended.

124
00:13:47,389 --> 00:13:55,681
You have now seen several complete workflows: real Arduino Blink,
a different output GPIO, a button input, browser-based

125
00:13:55,681 --> 00:14:00,884
analog control, voltage probing, and a passive circuit with no
code.

126
00:14:00,884 --> 00:14:09,746
For each new experiment, choose the correct execution mode, match
the code to the wiring, and inspect the actual electrical

127
00:14:09,746 --> 00:14:17,062
result. Stop before changing a running sketch, then run again.
Finally, keep a JSON backup.

128
00:14:17,062 --> 00:14:24,298
Start with a working example, change one idea at a time, and make
the result explainable.
